        "semanticLevel": "detail",
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='width:100%;height:100%;overflow:auto;box-sizing:border-box;container-type:inline-size;background:#eef4fb;font-family:system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#182d4c'><div style='box-sizing:border-box;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 clamp(20px,5cqw,40px) 64px'><div style='padding-top:32px'><header data-role='masthead'><div style='padding:10px 0;border-top:6px solid #294b83;border-bottom:1px solid #182d4c;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>PRIMITIVE · import, export</div><h1 style='margin:40px 0 0;font:850 clamp(36px,9.5cqw,72px)/.98 system-ui,sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.035em;text-wrap:balance;color:#182d4c'>Import and Export</h1><p style='margin:18px 0 0;max-width:36ch;font:400 clamp(20px,3.6cqw,26px)/1.4 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#46617f'>How one graph draws on another, and what it offers in return.</p></header></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:flex-start;gap:32px 40px'><div style='flex:999 1 380px;min-width:0'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Meaning</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><strong>What is this thing?</strong></p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>An import is where a graph uses something from another graph: a class, a set of classes, or a whole graph, with the permissions it has been granted over it. An export is where a graph offers something to whoever imports it. The two are the ends of a Bridge, and a Bridge is the connection between them.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Cross-graph coupling is never implicit. A custom node type used anywhere in a graph must trace back to an import that grants it. If it can&#39;t, that&#39;s not a documentation gap: the graph lacks the authority it&#39;s exercising.</p></section></div><div style='flex:1 1 220px;min-width:0'><section data-role='settings' style='padding-top:10px;border-top:3px solid #182d4c'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#182d4c'>At a glance</h2><dl style='margin:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:max-content 1fr;column-gap:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #a6bed9'><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#46617f'>Node types</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#182d4c;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>import, export</dd><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#46617f'>Shown when browsing</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#182d4c;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>No, only when editing</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#46617f'>Points at</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#182d4c;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>a class, a set of classes, or a whole graph</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#46617f'>On the border</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#182d4c;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>imports left, exports right</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#46617f'>Joined by</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#182d4c;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>a Bridge, drawn from an export to the import that uses it</dd></dl></section></div></div></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Invariants</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><strong>What makes this thing an import or an export, specifically?</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>A node is an <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>import</code> or an <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>export</code>, never both. Which end it is comes from its type, not a stored role. An import and an export are separate nodes even when they name the same <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>It names a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind</code>: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>node-class</code> (one custom node type), <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>library</code> (a bundle; an export of this kind with a class list is read by the create menu, and by nothing else — see Behavior), or <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>graph</code> (a whole graph, usually a template).</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>It names a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> — the concrete address of what it reaches.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>For <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: node-class</code>, the granted type key must actually be set. An import with a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> but no key grants nothing the validator can see, even though the UI may show it as configured.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Its <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>grants</code> say what&#39;s permitted over the target (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>read</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>create</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>update</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>execute</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>mutate</code>) — not every grant is meaningful for every <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The validator checks these</strong> (as warnings when a graph is loaded or saved, and as errors in its strict mode): an import or export with no <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>BRIDGE_REF_MISSING</code>); an import of a class with no <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>key</code> (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>BRIDGE_CLASS_KEY_MISSING</code>); and a node of a class, named by its <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.classRef</code>, in a graph that imports neither that class nor a graph or kit that hands classes on (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>CLASS_WITHOUT_IMPORT</code>). A class that has loaded for another graph does not count: a graph can use only the classes of its own imports.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Contract</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><strong>What data belongs to it?</strong></p><pre style='margin:0 0 16px;padding:14px 16px;overflow-x:auto;white-space:pre;background:#dce9f7;border-left:3px solid #294b83;font:400 13px/1.55 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#182d4c'>bridge   { ref, resourceKind, scope, grants[], key?, exposure?, entryPort? }</pre><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>One record, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.bridge</code>, and each fact once. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>key</code> is the class key an import of a class grants; <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exposure</code> is what an export offers; <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>entryPort</code> is the port an import enters by. Nothing else is stored: whether it is an import or an export is its type; the endpoint is the <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> and the entry port; there is no second record, and no copy of its settings at the top of <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data</code>. A loaded import carries those copies for the code that still reads them, and a saved one drops them. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/bridgeRecord.js</code> does both, and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>readBridgeSettings()</code> in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>bridgeSettings.js</code> is the one place that reads a record for the editor. On an export, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exposure.events</code> lists the events it offers; on an import, it lists the events it shows as ports.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Required at minimum: the node type (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>import</code> or <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>export</code>), <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code>. For <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: node-class</code>, also <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>key</code>.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>A node made from an imported class stores only its type (the class key) and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.classRef</code>, the address of the class. It carries no copy of the class and no record of the import it came through; the app finds both when the graph loads. Grants are a different contract.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Ports and Relationships</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>An import or an export states cross-graph authority and participation. Its structural root socket and authored class/resource contract are distinct from the root&#39;s presentation Surface chains. A Bridge carries no events today: a Method runs only through an <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>invokes</code> edge from an event port, in the same graph. Events across a Bridge are designed and not built (see Known gaps). The Bridge drawn in the workspace is derived from an import and the export it uses, not a new authority record.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Port/handle projections may be materialized by the runtime. Do not infer that every import is unconnected or that a Bridge line itself persists a grant.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Behavior</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><strong>What does it participate in?</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An <strong>import</strong> with <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: node-class</code> grants this graph permission to create instances of that type. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>getBridgeNodeClassKey()</code> in the validator reads it directly off the graph&#39;s own node list — no fetch, no recursion.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An import with <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: graph</code>, when dragged onto a canvas, resolves the target&#39;s surfaces into one bound node (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>deriveGraphBackedNodeArtifact()</code> in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>graphNodeContract.js</code>) — the target&#39;s ports and faces become that node&#39;s ports and faces.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An import with <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: graph</code> that points at a template reaches every class the template hands on. The template has one export of its own (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: graph</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> the template itself) whose <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exposure.nodeClasses</code> lists the class keys it offers. The classes are used in place: nothing is copied into the importing graph, which holds the one import and no more. Classes pass up the chain, so a template may list classes it reaches through its own import of another template.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An <strong>export</strong> says what this graph offers to a graph that imports it.<ul style='margin:6px 0 0;padding-left:22px;'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An export of the graph itself lists the classes it hands on. This is the current form.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An export that names a single class offers that class. This is the older form, and it still works.</li></ul></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>When both graphs are open in the same workspace, the import is drawn as one edge to the export it plugs into: the import on the left of its graph, the export on the right of the template. That edge is the Bridge; it is drawn from the two ends and is not stored.</li></ul><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An import that grants <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>execute</code> also lets a Method in this graph run a Method the template hands on. The template lists those on the same export (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exposure.methods</code>); the Method node here holds no source and names this import.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#182d4c'>Events</h3><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>An event runs the Methods its <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>invokes</code> edges lead to, in edge order, in the graph that announced it. It also reaches other open graphs across a Bridge.</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An export lists the events its graph offers (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exposure.events</code>, an allowlist of <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>&lt;emitter node id&gt;:&lt;event key&gt;</code>). Nothing is offered until it is listed.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An import lists the events it shows. Each one appears as a port on the import, named <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>event:&lt;emitter node id&gt;:&lt;event key&gt;</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>The importing graph draws an <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>invokes</code> edge from that port to one of its own Methods. When the emitter fires and both graphs are open, that Method runs in its own graph, with its own declared effects.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Only graphs that import the emitter are reached. Being open side by side is not enough. Each listener chooses by drawing its edge, and a Method that fails does not stop the others.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Known gaps</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Resource-kind support depends on the actual consumer. The library-kind reservation above remains a limitation, not a promised expansion path. Runtime delivery and browser connection behavior require their own smoke evidence.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><strong>Events across a Bridge: limits.</strong> Only graphs that are open are reached; a closed graph hears nothing. A Method an event runs for a graph that is not in focus may use only <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>updateNode</code>; any other change is refused so it cannot land on the focused graph. The emitting graph never learns who listens. An earlier design carried subscriptions on the import; it was retired on 2026-10-16 with the Event Handler node, and this replaces it.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Representations</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><strong>How is it represented?</strong></p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>An import or an export typically renders as a small authority card naming its end, resourceKind and target ref — never the target&#39;s own content. It points at what it grants or offers; it doesn&#39;t inline it. Collapsed, it shows just enough to answer &quot;what does this graph have authority over, and in which direction,&quot; without requiring a fetch of the target to render at all.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>An import or an export is edited on its own card. Which end it is comes from its type, so the editor shows it and does not change it: to turn one into the other, add the other and delete this. An import has a Setup tab (the address, what it takes, what it may do), an export has an Offers tab (the classes and Methods it hands on, and the other kinds of thing, closed until opened), and both have Events and Status tabs.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive</h2><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An import doesn&#39;t fetch or copy what it names. Resolving a ref into actual nodes is the resolver&#39;s job (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>GraphCrud.js</code>&#39;s <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>bridgeReference()</code>), triggered by an action on the import, not performed by the node as data.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>The Bridge drawn between an import and an export isn&#39;t stored, and it isn&#39;t a grant. It exists only while both graphs are open together (see Behavior).</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Neither end is a Portal. A Portal opens onto a graph; an import or an export grants authority over one. The two can point at the same <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> for entirely different reasons, and neither implies the other.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Older node-type dependency lists grant no authority. Imports and class bindings supply the current authority path.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: library</code> is a real, declared option in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>BRIDGE_KINDS</code>, and the create menu reads one: an export of this kind hands on the classes its <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exposure</code> lists. The validator&#39;s authority check does not read it, exactly as it would not read an unkeyed <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>node-class</code> import. Treat it as partly supported; an export of the graph itself (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: graph</code>) with a class list is the form to author.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Neither end is a Fragment. A Fragment is a piece of a node; an import grants authority over something that is not part of the importing node.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Examples</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Try it:</p><ol style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:26px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Switch to edit mode (the pencil). Imports and exports are shown only while you edit.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Press Add node and choose Import. A card appears.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Select it and press Edit, or double-click it. On the Setup tab, give it the address of the graph it uses, say what it takes (a whole graph, or one class) and what it may do with it: read, create, update or execute.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>In the graph that offers something, add an Export. On its Offers tab, tick what it offers: the classes it holds, and the Methods that hold source. Nothing is offered until it is ticked.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Open both graphs in the workspace. The import is drawn joined to the export it uses. That line is the Bridge.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>To hear an event across it, tick the event on the export’s Events tab. The import shows it as a port on its own Events tab, and the importing graph draws an <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>invokes</code> edge from that port to a Method of its own.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>The Status tab says what the Bridge resolves to: what the other graph offers, and how much of it this import can reach.</li></ol><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><strong>Examples live outside the primitive, referenced, not embedded.</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>templates/page-template/root.node</code> — four imports for its own classes (Masthead, Route, Settings, Section) and one export for the template, listing all four. A graph that uses it holds a single <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: graph</code> import of the template and can create all four.</li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>The Page Template is linked from this page.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#294b83'>Where this lives</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Source (twilite repository; the files still carry the old name):</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/importExport.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/bridgeSettings.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/eventBridge.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/bridgeEvents.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/components/BridgeEventsEditorSection.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/graphNodeContract.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/bridgeEditing.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/Nodes/BridgeInlineEditor.js</code></li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'>Focused tests:</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 system-ui,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;color:#253c58'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/bridgeSettings.test.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#dce9f7;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/bridgeEvents.test.js</code></li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:40px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:stretch;gap:28px 32px'><div style='flex:1 1 190px;min-width:0'><section data-role='route' data-prominence='primary' style='height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;display:flex;flex-direction:column;padding-top:12px;border-top:3px solid #294b83'><h2 style='margin:0 0 8px;font:750 26px/1.1 system-ui;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#182d4c'>Page Template</h2><p style='margin:0 0 10px;font:400 14px/1.55 system-ui;color:#46617f'>A template with four class imports and one export.</p><a data-role='route-link' href='tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/root.node' style='margin-top:auto;font:700 13px/20px system-ui;text-decoration:none;color:#294b83'>Open the template →</a></section></div></div></div></div></div>"
        },
        "memo": "",
        "link": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bridge-summary",
      "type": "face",
      "label": "Bridge summary",
      "position": {
        "x": -150,
        "y": -729
      },
      "width": 300,
      "height": 200,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270
        },
        {
          "id": "sequence-in",
          "key": "sequence-in",
          "label": "sequence-in",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180
        },
        {
          "id": "sequence-out",
          "key": "sequence-out",
          "label": "sequence-out",
          "direction": "output",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 0
        }
      ],
      "handles": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root"
        },
        {
          "id": "sequence-in",
          "key": "sequence-in",
          "label": "sequence-in",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180,
          "portId": "sequence-in"
        },
        {
          "id": "sequence-out",
          "key": "sequence-out",
          "label": "sequence-out",
          "direction": "output",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 0,
          "portId": "sequence-out"
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "fe331a1c-0790-447b-ad05-83ddc1898214"
        },
        "view": {
          "intent": "node",
          "payload": "node.web.summary"
        },
        "semanticLevel": "summary",
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='container-type:inline-size;width:100%;height:100%'><div style='box-sizing:border-box;height:100%;padding:5cqw;background:#eef4fb;border:1px solid #a6bed9;border-top:2cqw solid #294b83;color:#182d4c;font-family:system-ui;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2cqw;overflow:hidden'><div style='font:750 3.6cqw/1.15 ui-monospace,monospace;letter-spacing:.1em;color:#294b83'>PRIMITIVE / IMPORT-AND-EXPORT</div><div style='font:800 11cqw/1.02 system-ui;overflow-wrap:anywhere;display:-webkit-box;-webkit-line-clamp:2;-webkit-box-orient:vertical;overflow:hidden'>Import and Export</div><div style='font:550 4.8cqw/1.23 system-ui;color:#46617f;display:-webkit-box;-webkit-line-clamp:3;-webkit-box-orient:vertical;overflow:hidden'>How one graph draws on another, and what it offers in return.</div><div style='margin-top:auto;padding-top:1.5cqw;border-top:1px solid #a6bed9;font:700 3.4cqw/1.15 ui-monospace,monospace;color:#294b83'>10 SECTIONS / LANGUAGE CONTRACT</div></div></div>"
