    {
      "id": "bridge-behavior",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Behavior",
      "position": {
        "x": 3000,
        "y": -350
      },
      "width": 720,
      "height": 380,
      "data": {
        "title": "Behavior",
        "body": "**What does it participate in?**\n\n- An **import** with `resourceKind: node-class` grants this graph permission to create instances of that type. `getBridgeNodeClassKey()` in the validator reads it directly off the graph's own node list — no fetch, no recursion.\n- An import with `resourceKind: graph`, when dragged onto a canvas, resolves the target's surfaces into one bound node (`deriveGraphBackedNodeArtifact()` in `graphNodeContract.js`) — the target's ports and faces become that node's ports and faces.\n- An import with `resourceKind: graph` that points at a template reaches every class the template hands on. The template has one export of its own (`resourceKind: graph`, `ref` the template itself) whose `exposure.nodeClasses` lists the class keys it offers. The classes are used in place: nothing is copied into the importing graph, which holds the one import and no more. Classes pass up the chain, so a template may list classes it reaches through its own import of another template.\n- An **export** says what this graph offers to a graph that imports it.\n  - An export of the graph itself lists the classes it hands on. This is the current form.\n  - An export that names a single class offers that class. This is the older form, and it still works.\n- When both graphs are open in the same workspace, the import is drawn as one edge to the export it plugs into: the import on the left of its graph, the export on the right of the template. That edge is the Bridge; it is drawn from the two ends and is not stored.\n\n- An import that grants `execute` also lets a Method in this graph run a Method the template hands on. The template lists those on the same export (`exposure.methods`); the Method node here holds no source and names this import.\n\n## Events\n\nAn event runs the Methods its `invokes` edges lead to, in edge order, in the graph that announced it. It also reaches other open graphs across a Bridge.\n\n- An export lists the events its graph offers (`exposure.events`, an allowlist of `<emitter node id>:<event key>`). Nothing is offered until it is listed.\n- An import lists the events it shows. Each one appears as a port on the import, named `event:<emitter node id>:<event key>`.\n- The importing graph draws an `invokes` edge from that port to one of its own Methods. When the emitter fires and both graphs are open, that Method runs in its own graph, with its own declared effects.\n- Only graphs that import the emitter are reached. Being open side by side is not enough. Each listener chooses by drawing its edge, and a Method that fails does not stop the others.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "bridge",
          "updatedAt": 1791592047778,
          "reason": "bridge-reference"
        },
        "identity": {
