    {
      "id": "bridge-boundary",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive",
      "position": {
        "x": 1320,
        "y": 150
      },
      "width": 720,
      "height": 380,
      "data": {
        "title": "Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive",
        "body": "- An import doesn't fetch or copy what it names. Resolving a ref into actual nodes is the resolver's job (`GraphCrud.js`'s `bridgeReference()`), triggered by an action on the import, not performed by the node as data.\n- The Bridge drawn between an import and an export isn't stored, and it isn't a grant. It exists only while both graphs are open together (see Behavior).\n- Neither end is a Portal. A Portal opens onto a graph; an import or an export grants authority over one. The two can point at the same `ref` for entirely different reasons, and neither implies the other.\n- Older node-type dependency lists grant no authority. Imports and class bindings supply the current authority path.\n- `resourceKind: library` is a real, declared option in `BRIDGE_KINDS`, and the create menu reads one: an export of this kind hands on the classes its `exposure` lists. The validator's authority check does not read it, exactly as it would not read an unkeyed `node-class` import. Treat it as partly supported; an export of the graph itself (`resourceKind: graph`) with a class list is the form to author.\n- Neither end is a Fragment. A Fragment is a piece of a node; an import grants authority over something that is not part of the importing node.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "bridge",
          "updatedAt": 1791592047780,
          "reason": "bridge-reference"
        },
        "identity": {
