    {
      "id": "bridge-examples",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Examples",
      "position": {
        "x": 2160,
        "y": 150
      },
      "width": 720,
      "height": 380,
      "data": {
        "title": "Examples",
        "body": "Try it:\n1. Switch to edit mode (the pencil). Imports and exports are shown only while you edit.\n2. Press Add node and choose Import. A card appears.\n3. Select it and press Edit, or double-click it. On the Setup tab, give it the address of the graph it uses, say what it takes (a whole graph, or one class) and what it may do with it: read, create, update or execute.\n4. In the graph that offers something, add an Export. On its Offers tab, tick what it offers: the classes it holds, and the Methods that hold source. Nothing is offered until it is ticked.\n5. Open both graphs in the workspace. The import is drawn joined to the export it uses. That line is the Bridge.\n6. To hear an event across it, tick the event on the export’s Events tab. The import shows it as a port on its own Events tab, and the importing graph draws an `invokes` edge from that port to a Method of its own.\n7. The Status tab says what the Bridge resolves to: what the other graph offers, and how much of it this import can reach.\n\n**Examples live outside the primitive, referenced, not embedded.**\n\n- `templates/page-template/root.node` — four imports for its own classes (Masthead, Route, Settings, Section) and one export for the template, listing all four. A graph that uses it holds a single `resourceKind: graph` import of the template and can create all four.\n\n\nThe Page Template is linked from this page.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "bridge",
          "updatedAt": 1791592047782,
          "reason": "bridge-reference"
        },
